    "record": {
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e9a5f727-046e-5c6d-b3a8-b57aa0e0de57",
        ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-6,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $b$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $3$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b-c.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y={t}^{2}-6.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=(-3)^{2}-6=9-6=3.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $y={t}^{2}-6.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb={0}^{2}-6=0-6=-6.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $x=t+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=3+1=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b-c=3-(-6)-4=5.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
